      <h2 class="lp-section-h2">What Makes La Grange Masonry <em>Different</em></h2>
      <div class="lp-profile-grid">
        <div class="lp-profile-card">
          <div class="lp-profile-card-label">Primary Building Era</div>
          <div class="lp-profile-card-value">1900s – 1950s</div>
          <p class="lp-profile-card-desc">La Grange spans 1900s–1920s Craftsman bungalows, 1920s–1940s Georgian colonials, and postwar construction. Each era used different mortar — era identification is the first and most critical step before any repair in zip codes 60525/60526.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="lp-profile-card">
          <div class="lp-profile-card-label">Dominant Brick Type</div>
          <div class="lp-profile-card-value">Chicago Common &amp; Buff and Red Clay</div>
          <p class="lp-profile-card-desc">Pre-war Chicago Common, buff, and red clay brick all require lime-based mortar. Portland cement on these materials traps moisture and destroys the original brick face — the most common contractor error in Cook County.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="lp-profile-card">
          <div class="lp-profile-card-label">Climate Risk</div>
          <div class="lp-profile-card-value">High — 90+ Freeze-Thaw Cycles/Year</div>
          <p class="lp-profile-card-desc">90+ annual Cook County freeze-thaw cycles push moisture into failing mortar year after year — accelerating crumbling, spalling, and water infiltration on La Grange's historic brick.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- COMMON MASONRY PROBLEMS -->
  <section class="lp-section lp-problems" style="background:#F5F3EF;border-top:1px solid #D8D2C8;">
    <div class="lp-section-inner">
      <div class="lp-section-label">Common Masonry Problems in La Grange</div>
      <h2 class="lp-section-h2">What We Fix — <em>Most Often in La Grange</em></h2>
      <div class="lp-problems-grid">
        <div class="lp-problem-card">
          <div class="lp-problem-accent"></div>
          <h3>Historic Lime Mortar Failure on Pre-War Facades</h3>
          <p>La Grange's pre-war lime putty mortar has failed in exposed joints after 80–120 years. These require lime-based tuckpointing — Portland cement on soft historic Cook County brick causes irreversible spalling that can't be undone.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="lp-problem-card">
          <div class="lp-problem-accent"></div>
          <h3>Commercial Facade Brick Joint Deterioration</h3>
          <p>Downtown La Grange facades carry decades of mismatched repair mortar from multiple contractors — creating visual and structural inconsistency. Our assessment identifies every mortar type and develops a systematic protocol for full restoration.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="lp-problem-card">
          <div class="lp-problem-accent"></div>
          <h3>Chimney Reconstruction on Century-Old Homes</h3>
          <p>La Grange's oldest chimneys are now 100–120 years old — some requiring partial or full reconstruction. We assess before recommending repair vs. rebuild, always starting with the most conservative intervention that resolves the problem.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="lp-problem-card">
          <div class="lp-problem-accent"></div>
          <h3>Efflorescence and Water Infiltration on East and North Walls</h3>
          <p>East and north walls on La Grange homes face the most moisture exposure. White efflorescence signals active water movement through mortar joints — tuckpointing closes the source; cleaning alone never stops the infiltration.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

